#: ../../../datastructures.md:1
msgid "Data Structures Tutorial"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:3
msgid ""
"This tutorial gives you a quick introduction to the most common use cases "
"and default behaviour of xlwings when reading and writing values. For an in-"
"depth documentation of how to control the behavior using the `options` "
"method, have a look at [Converters and Options](converters.md#converters-and-"
"options)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:7
msgid "All code samples below depend on the following import:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:13
msgid "Single Cells"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:14
msgid ""
"Single cells are by default returned either as `float`, `unicode`, `None` or "
"`datetime` objects, depending on whether the cell contains a number, a "
"string, is empty or represents a date:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:33
msgid "Lists"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:34
msgid ""
"1d lists: Ranges that represent rows or columns in Excel are returned as "
"simple lists, which means that once they are in Python, you've lost the "
"information about the orientation. If that is an issue, the next point shows "
"you how to preserve this info:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:48
msgid "To force a single cell to arrive as list, use:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:56
msgid ""
"To write a list in column orientation to Excel, use `transpose`: `sheet."
"range('A1').options(transpose=True).value = [1,2,3,4]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:59
msgid ""
"2d lists: If the row or column orientation has to be preserved, set `ndim` "
"in the Range options. This will return the Ranges as nested lists (\"2d "
"lists\"):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:69
msgid ""
"2 dimensional Ranges are automatically returned as nested lists. When "
"assigning (nested) lists to a Range in Excel, it's enough to just specify "
"the top left cell as target address. This sample also makes use of index "
"notation to read the values back into Python:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:80
msgid ""
"Try to minimize the number of interactions with Excel. It is always more "
"efficient to do `sheet.range('A1').value = [[1,2],[3,4]]` than `sheet."
"range('A1').value = [1, 2]` and `sheet.range('A2').value = [3, 4]`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:84
msgid "Range expanding"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:86
msgid ""
"You can get the dimensions of Excel Ranges dynamically through either the "
"method `expand` or through the `expand` keyword in the `options` method. "
"While `expand` gives back an expanded Range object, options are only "
"evaluated when accessing the values of a Range. The difference is best "
"explained with an example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:106
msgid ""
"`'table'` expands to `'down'` and `'right'`, the other available options "
"which can be used for column or row only expansion, respectively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:110
msgid ""
"Using `expand()` together with a named Range as top left cell gives you a "
"flexible setup in Excel: You can move around the table and change its size "
"without having to adjust your code, e.g. by using something like `sheet."
"range('NamedRange').expand().value`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:115
msgid "NumPy arrays"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:117
msgid ""
"NumPy arrays work similar to nested lists. However, empty cells are "
"represented by `nan` instead of `None`. If you want to read in a Range as "
"array, set `convert=np.array` in the `options` method:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:130
msgid "Pandas DataFrames"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:149
msgid "Pandas Series"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:176
msgid ""
"You only need to specify the top left cell when writing a list, a NumPy "
"array or a Pandas DataFrame to Excel, e.g.: `sheet['A1'].value = np.eye(10)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:180
msgid "Chunking: Read/Write big DataFrames etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../datastructures.md:182
msgid ""
"When you read and write from or to big ranges, you may have to chunk them or "
"you will hit a timeout or a memory error. The ideal `chunksize` will depend "
"on your system and size of the array, so you will have to try out a few "
"different chunksizes to find one that works well:"
msgstr ""

